      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Different perspectives exist on state control over the internet. Some argue that national sovereignty necessitates the right to regulate content and restrict access to safeguard national security, protect citizens from disinformation, and maintain social order. Conversely, proponents of a free and open internet argue that excessive state control constitutes censorship, infringes on fundamental human rights, and stifles innovation. Finally, others advocate for hybrid models that balance security concerns with the principles of open governance, often through localized regulation tied to international norms.",
